  "id": "Compound::DB00808 - Gene::760",
  "verbalization": "Indapamide is implicated in the relationship with calcium (Ca2) through a series of mechanistic pathways that converge on the enzyme carbonate dehydratase. The evidence suggests that Indapamide binds to Ca12, which then participates in carbonate dehydratase activity, ultimately leading to the interaction with Ca2. This pathway is supported by multiple visible paths, all of which point to a direct or indirect link between Indapamide and calcium, with the lowest common ancestor of the entities indicating a shared classification as thiazide diuretics and organonitrogen heterocyclic compounds. The scores from the paths suggest a consistent and plausible mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction between Indapamide and Ca2 remains somewhat indirect, as the evidence does not directly specify the binding affinity or the precise molecular mechanism. The integration of these paths supports the hypothesis that Indapamide influences calcium homeostasis through a metabolic pathway involving carbonate dehydratase, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct role of Indapamide in Ca2 regulation.",
